WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.080
What are strings, languages, and

2
00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.060
the empty string? These are

3
00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:06.080
the objects every automaton will

4
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.420
read and classify. Confuse a

5
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:09.280
string with a set of

6
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:10.860
strings, and your beautiful proof

7
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.320
falls apart before it even

8
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:12.660
begins.

9
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.100
An alphabet is a finite,

10
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.780
nonempty set of symbols, usually

11
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.920
called Sigma. For this lesson,

12
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.900
choose the alphabet containing zero

13
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.840
and one. Those are symbols,

14
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:25.780
not numerical values that we

15
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:26.520
are about to add.

16
00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:28.500
A string is a finite

17
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.220
sequence of symbols from that

18
00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.260
alphabet. Take one, then zero,

19
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.740
then one, and place them

20
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:35.960
in that order to build

21
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.600
the string one zero one.

22
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.520
Repetition is allowed, and order

23
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:40.880
matters.

24
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:42.700
The length of a string

25
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.700
counts its symbols, not the

26
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.940
number it might represent. One

27
00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.880
zero one contains three symbols,

28
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.600
so its length is three.

29
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:52.760
Even a string beginning with

30
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:54.400
zero keeps that zero in

31
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:54.900
its length.

32
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.200
Now remove every symbol, leaving

33
00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.620
a sequence of length zero.

34
00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:01.880
That sequence is the empty

35
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.220
string, written epsilon. Epsilon names

36
00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.980
the empty sequence; it is

37
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:07.400
not another symbol card hiding

38
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.020
in the alphabet.

39
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.020
Do not confuse the empty

40
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.360
string with the string containing

41
00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.260
a single zero. The first

42
00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:14.600
has length zero, while the

43
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.600
second has length one. Blank

44
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.000
space and a zero symbol

45
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.020
are rather different things, obviously.

46
00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.060
Concatenation joins strings end to

47
00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:24.960
end without rearranging their symbols.

48
00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.080
Let x be one zero,

49
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.480
and let y be one.

50
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.360
Move y after x to

51
00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.140
obtain x y, the string

52
00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:33.220
one zero one.

53
00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.540
Reversing that order gives y

54
00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.480
x, which is one one

55
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.960
zero. So concatenation is generally

56
00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.280
not commutative: changing the order

57
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.340
can change the string. Its

58
00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.380
length is additive, because two

59
00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.040
symbols followed by one symbol

60
00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.060
gives three symbols.

61
00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.380
What happens when we concatenate

62
00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:52.860
epsilon with one zero one?

63
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.360
No symbol is added, so

64
00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:56.520
the result is still one

65
00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.120
zero one, whether epsilon comes

66
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.160
first or last. This is

67
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:02.680
why epsilon is the identity

68
00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.160
for string concatenation.

69
00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:06.480
A language is a set

70
00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:07.960
of strings over an alphabet.

71
00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.620
For example, let L contain

72
00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.160
exactly the strings zero one

73
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.000
and one zero. The braces

74
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.700
collect two words into a

75
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.300
set; they are not symbols

76
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.740
inside either word.

77
00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.100
A set ignores the order

78
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.220
in which its members are

79
00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.060
listed, and listing a member

80
00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.940
twice adds nothing. So reversing

81
00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:29.220
the two entries in L

82
00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.800
leaves the language unchanged. Notice

83
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.400
the contrast: rearranging members of

84
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.120
a language is harmless, but

85
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.140
rearranging symbols inside a string

86
00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:39.280
may change the word.

87
00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.760
Sigma star means all finite

88
00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.560
strings over Sigma, including epsilon.

89
00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.440
Over our binary alphabet, list

90
00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.360
epsilon first, then zero and

91
00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.340
one, then zero zero, zero

92
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:51.600
one, one zero, and one

93
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.720
one. Continue through longer lengths

94
00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:56.720
forever, although every individual string

95
00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.060
still has finite length.

96
00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:00.260
Our language L has only

97
00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.200
two strings, while Sigma star

98
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.700
contains infinitely many. A language

99
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.400
can therefore be finite or

100
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.680
infinite. Every language over Sigma

101
00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:09.920
is a subset of Sigma

102
00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:10.300
star.

103
00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:13.580
Now compare three different objects:

104
00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.700
epsilon, the empty language, and

105
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:18.760
the language containing only epsilon.

106
00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.200
Epsilon is one string with

107
00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.500
length zero. The empty language

108
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.460
has no members, while the

109
00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:28.100
language containing epsilon has exactly

110
00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:28.820
one member.

111
00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:32.180
Consequently, epsilon belongs to the

112
00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:34.540
language containing epsilon, but does

113
00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:35.780
not belong to the empty

114
00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.420
language. The membership symbol asks

115
00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.000
whether a string is an

116
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.740
element of a language. The

117
00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:44.000
subset symbol instead compares sets,

118
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.740
as when we say L

119
00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.080
is a subset of Sigma

120
00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:47.380
star.

121
00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.200
We can concatenate languages too,

122
00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.840
by concatenating every allowed pair

123
00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.420
of strings. Concatenating L with

124
00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:57.300
the language containing only epsilon

125
00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.800
returns L, because epsilon leaves

126
00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.180
each string unchanged. Concatenating L

127
00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:04.700
with the empty language returns

128
00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:06.480
the empty language, because there

129
00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:07.920
is no second string to

130
00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:08.320
choose.

131
00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:10.820
For any language, star allows

132
00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:12.900
concatenations of zero or more

133
00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.160
of its words. Choosing zero

134
00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.240
words produces epsilon, even when

135
00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:18.500
the language has no words

136
00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:20.740
to choose. Therefore, the star

137
00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:22.100
of the empty language is

138
00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:24.160
the language containing only epsilon,

139
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:25.920
not the empty language.

140
00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:28.020
Things to take note of:

141
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.000
a symbol is one alphabet

142
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.720
item, a string is a

143
00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:33.580
finite sequence, and a language

144
00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:34.500
is a set of strings.

145
00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:37.280
Epsilon has length zero, but

146
00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:39.160
the language containing epsilon has

147
00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.480
one member. String order matters,

148
00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:43.360
set listing order does not,

149
00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.040
and star always includes epsilon.

150
00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:48.420
Quick check: is the statement

151
00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.000
that epsilon belongs to the

152
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.740
empty language true or false?

153
00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:53.420
It is false, because an

154
00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:55.060
empty set contains no strings

155
00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:57.080
at all. But epsilon does

156
00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:58.280
belong to the star of

157
00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.260
the empty language, because zero

158
00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:01.560
repetitions are allowed.

159
00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:04.300
Before moving on, write one

160
00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.400
string, one language containing it,

161
00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:07.780
and that language as a

162
00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:09.640
subset of Sigma star. Then

163
00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.080
explain why concatenating with epsilon

164
00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.160
preserves a string, while choosing

165
00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:15.460
a partner from an empty

166
00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:17.940
language is impossible. Keep those

167
00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:19.780
object types straight, and the

168
00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:21.860
automata lessons become much easier

169
00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:22.420
to follow.
